					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/fooloftime" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1251998); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1251998&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608349718" alt="fooloftime" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1251998)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/fooloftime" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1251998); return false;" >fooloftime</a></span> <span class="odate time_1324519979 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">22 Dec 2011 02:12</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1333073">
									

<p>I&#8230; don't like it that much. Not sure how to put it in words. It's not that it is badly written. More that&#8230; well the original Splinters tale was really good, so it could be initial expectations set to high, but more than that Splinters felt like an actual series of events that had occured. This meanwhile feels like a James Bond novel. It has so many tropes. The Girl Who Couldn't Get Over Her Father's Disappearance. The Torture Scene. The Villainess, fighting against The Organisation. The Alias. The Chess Metaphor. She's even set up to be Impossible Brilliant, yet Broken.</p>
<p>I really preferred the initial view I had of this. No broken genius, just a realistically competent researcher, following up on her fathers work, unknowingly heading straight for horrific revelations or something equally nasty.</p>
<p>Going beyond just personal preference&#8230; if you want to set her up as a mastermind&#8230; maybe make things harder for her. Masterminds are generally measured by the opposition they face.</p>
<p>Other than that I think it's good.</p>

							</div>

								
					
